# Line endings are settled here — once, for the whole repository — and not by
# each machine's `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol`. Those live outside the repo (on
# Windows the Git installer writes `core.autocrlf=true` into the system config),
# so without this file the checked-out bytes depend on who cloned where: the
# index holds LF, a Windows working tree holds CRLF, and the moment that
# per-machine setting is off, `git status` reports every text file as modified
# with identical insertion and deletion counts and no content change.
#
# Convention: LF in the index AND LF in the working tree, on every platform.
# See CLAUDE.md §6.4.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs. binary and normalize text to LF on the
# way in; `eol=lf` pins the checkout side to LF as well, so index and working
# tree agree byte-for-byte everywhere. Binary types below are listed explicitly
# anyway — content detection is a heuristic, and a mangled `.aseprite` fixture
# is a silently broken test, not a compile error.

*               text=auto eol=lf

# Binary payloads — byte-exact, never line-ending-converted, never diffed as text.
*.aseprite      binary
*.ase           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.wasm          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
